            {
              "id": "CVE-2021-47110-linux-libc-dev",
              "name": "DpkgMatcherExactIndirectMatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2021-47110 high vulnerability for linux-libc-dev package"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:  x86/kvm: Disable kvmclock on all CPUs on shutdown  Currenly, we disable kvmclock from machine_shutdown() hook and this only happens for boot CPU. We need to disable it for all CPUs to guard against memory corruption e.g. on restore from hibernate.  Note, writing '0' to kvmclock MSR doesn't clear memory location, it just prevents hypervisor from updating the location so for the short while after write and while CPU is still alive, the clock remains usable and correct so we don't need to switch to some other clocksource."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/anchore/grype",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2021-47110\nSeverity: high\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nVersion: 4.19.289-2\nFix Version: \nType: deb\nLocation: /usr/share/doc/linux-libc-dev/copyright\nData Namespace: debian:distro:debian:10\nLink: [CVE-2021-47110](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2021-47110)",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2021-47110**\n| Severity | Package | Version | Fix Version | Type | Location | Data Namespace | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| high  | linux-libc-dev  | 4.19.289-2  |   | deb  | /usr/share/doc/linux-libc-dev/copyright  | debian:distro:debian:10  | [CVE-2021-47110](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2021-47110)  |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.1"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2021-47112-linux-libc-dev",
              "name": "DpkgMatcherExactIndirectMatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2021-47112 medium vulnerability for linux-libc-dev package"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:  x86/kvm: Teardown PV features on boot CPU as well  Various PV features (Async PF, PV EOI, steal time) work through memory shared with hypervisor and when we restore from hibernation we must properly teardown all these features to make sure hypervisor doesn't write to stale locations after we jump to the previously hibernated kernel (which can try to place anything there). For secondary CPUs the job is already done by kvm_cpu_down_prepare(), register syscore ops to do the same for boot CPU."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/anchore/grype",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2021-47112\nSeverity: medium\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nVersion: 4.19.289-2\nFix Version: \nType: deb\nLocation: /usr/share/doc/linux-libc-dev/copyright\nData Namespace: debian:distro:debian:10\nLink: [CVE-2021-47112](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2021-47112)",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2021-47112**\n| Severity | Package | Version | Fix Version | Type | Location | Data Namespace | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| medium  | linux-libc-dev  | 4.19.289-2  |   | deb  | /usr/share/doc/linux-libc-dev/copyright  | debian:distro:debian:10  | [CVE-2021-47112](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2021-47112)  |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2021-47113-linux-libc-dev",
              "name": "DpkgMatcherExactIndirectMatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2021-47113 medium vulnerability for linux-libc-dev package"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:  btrfs: abort in rename_exchange if we fail to insert the second ref  Error injection stress uncovered a problem where we'd leave a dangling inode ref if we failed during a rename_exchange.  This happens because we insert the inode ref for one side of the rename, and then for the other side.  If this second inode ref insert fails we'll leave the first one dangling and leave a corrupt file system behind.  Fix this by aborting if we did the insert for the first inode ref."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/anchore/grype",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2021-47113\nSeverity: medium\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nVersion: 4.19.289-2\nFix Version: \nType: deb\nLocation: /usr/share/doc/linux-libc-dev/copyright\nData Namespace: debian:distro:debian:10\nLink: [CVE-2021-47113](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2021-47113)",
