              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2023-50387-libsystemd0",
              "name": "DpkgMatcherExactIndirectMatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2023-50387 high vulnerability for libsystemd0 package"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Certain DNSSEC aspects of the DNS protocol (in RFC 4033, 4034, 4035, 6840, and related RFCs) allow remote attackers to cause a denial of service (CPU consumption) via one or more DNSSEC responses, aka the \"KeyTrap\" issue. One of the concerns is that, when there is a zone with many DNSKEY and RRSIG records, the protocol specification implies that an algorithm must evaluate all combinations of DNSKEY and RRSIG records."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/anchore/grype",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2023-50387\nSeverity: high\nPackage: libsystemd0\nVersion: 241-7~deb10u10\nFix Version: \nType: deb\nLocation: /usr/share/doc/libsystemd0/copyright\nData Namespace: debian:distro:debian:10\nLink: [CVE-2023-50387](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2023-50387)",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2023-50387**\n| Severity | Package | Version | Fix Version | Type | Location | Data Namespace | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| high  | libsystemd0  | 241-7~deb10u10  |   | deb  | /usr/share/doc/libsystemd0/copyright  | debian:distro:debian:10  | [CVE-2023-50387](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2023-50387)  |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2023-50387-libudev1",
              "name": "DpkgMatcherExactIndirectMatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2023-50387 high vulnerability for libudev1 package"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Certain DNSSEC aspects of the DNS protocol (in RFC 4033, 4034, 4035, 6840, and related RFCs) allow remote attackers to cause a denial of service (CPU consumption) via one or more DNSSEC responses, aka the \"KeyTrap\" issue. One of the concerns is that, when there is a zone with many DNSKEY and RRSIG records, the protocol specification implies that an algorithm must evaluate all combinations of DNSKEY and RRSIG records."
              },
